<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The launcher icon's foreground: a t-shirt.

    Drawn here rather than imported because there was nothing to import. Every file
    in assets/images is a 1x1 transparent placeholder — the Expo template's, never
    replaced — so the app that ships has no icon either, and generating densities
    from those would have produced one blank PNG per density.

    A filled silhouette, and a garment rather than a wardrobe or a hanger, because
    the size that matters is 48dp. A cupboard at 48dp is a grey rectangle; a hanger
    is worse, because its hook comes out about three pixels across and reads as a
    blob on a stick — which is what the first draft of this file did. A shape with
    an outline and a collar notch still reads at that size.

    The 108x108 viewport is the adaptive-icon canvas. Only the middle 72x72 is
    guaranteed to be visible — the launcher masks the rest and may animate within
    it — so the artwork stays inside 18..90 on both axes.

    Keep this in step with scripts/generate-launcher-icons.py, which draws the same
    outline for the Android 7 rasters.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <path
        android:pathData="M42,26 L30,30 L22,46 L34,52 L34,82 L74,82 L74,52 L86,46 L78,30 L66,26 C62,34 46,34 42,26 Z"
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFF" />
</vector>
